/**
 * @page events_subscription_publish Event Subscription and Publish
 *
 * @brief Describes how components subscribe to events and how events are
 * published and delivered to subscribers.
 *
 * ## Event-Driven Communication
 *
 * The kernel uses an event-driven communication model to allow components
 * to communicate without requiring direct dependencies on one another.
 *
 * An event producer publishes an event, while interested components subscribe
 * to receive notifications about that event.
 *
 * ## Event Subscription
 *
 * A component subscribes to an event to indicate that it is interested in
 * receiving notifications when that event is published.
 *
 * A subscription typically associates an event identifier with a callback
 * that handles the event.
 *
 * @code
 * // Conceptual example:
 * // Subscribe a callback to an event.
 * _kernel_subEvt(EVENT_ID, event_handler);
 * @endcode
 *
 * The callback is invoked by the event delivery mechanism when a matching
 * event is published.
 *
 * ## Event Publishing
 *
 * An event producer publishes an event when something relevant happens in
 * the system.
 *
 * Publishing an event allows all registered subscribers for that event to
 * be notified without the producer needing to know which components are
 * listening.
 *
 * @code
 * // Conceptual example:
 * // Publish an event.
 * _kernel_pubEvt(EVENT_ID, event_data);
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Event Delivery Flow
 *
 * The typical event flow consists of the following steps:
 *
 * - A component subscribes to an event and provides an event handler.
 * - The event producer detects a condition or state change.
 * - The producer publishes the corresponding event.
 * - The kernel event mechanism identifies the matching subscribers.
 * - The registered handlers are invoked with the published event data.
 * - Subscribers process the notification independently.
 *
 * ## Decoupling Components
 *
 * Event subscription and publishing separate event producers from event
 * consumers:
 *
 * - Producers only need to know which event to publish.
 * - Consumers only need to subscribe to events they are interested in.
 * - Multiple consumers can react to the same event.
 * - Producers do not need direct references to consumer components.
 *
 * ## Implementation Guidelines
 *
 * - Keep event handlers focused on processing the notification.
 * - Avoid unnecessary work in event handlers if callbacks execute in a
 *   time-sensitive context.
 * - Define and document the meaning and structure of event data.
 * - Ensure that subscriptions are valid for the lifetime of their handlers.
 *
 * @note The function names and code snippets above are conceptual examples.
 *       Refer to the kernel event API for the actual subscription and
 *       publishing macros, function signatures, callback conventions, and
 *       event data ownership rules.
 *
 * @see \ref kernel_sgls
 * @see \ref kernel_arch
 */